ve like a few major story events  in the course of 10 episodes and why is  this a bad thing you may ask well that's  because you need to have a good balance  of build up and payoff and to also have  enough time for your characters to grow  a prime example of perfect pacing is in  season 2 where throughout the king's  landing subplot tyrion is learning how  to be a great hand while also being  tasked with taking on the massive  obstacle of defending king's landing  from stannis  that is proper build-up to a major story  event that thoroughly develops turing as  a character but if the king's landing  subplot was similar to season 7 we would  have tyrion defending the kingdom having  to marry sansa and then being accused of  joffrey's death and being tried for it  this is how season seven is for daenerys  she arrives to dragonstone goes through  numerous battles meets the king of the  north and falls in love with him has to  save everyone from the white hunt and  meets with cersei for a ceasefire all in  the course of one season  that's pretty nuts  and any scenes we get with her that  includes dialogue is mostly tyrion  saying repetitive stuff like don't burn  down king's landing forehead and don't  use your dragons at all you'd be flying  around the open seas alone for who knows  how long i wouldn't be alone  i would have drogon viserion and rhaegal  what can anyone do to them which always  leads to the same outcome of dany hating  tyrion and ignoring his idiotic advice  that's not character development  actually think about it how does  daenerys change as a character  throughout season seven really the only  major change to her is her falling in  love with jon snow and seemingly  becoming more compassionate  and again that's extremely rushed you're  telling me these two people fell in love  in practically four scenes  along with jon snow being one of the  main reasons her dragon got killed  going from daenerys being the most  depressed she's probably ever felt at  t